--- !u!114 &255716618
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 255716615}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5f7201a12d95ffc409449d95f23cf332, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_FontData:
    m_Font: {fileID: 10102, guid: 0000000000000000e000000000000000, type: 0}
    m_FontSize: 14
    m_FontStyle: 0
    m_BestFit: 0
    m_MinSize: 10
    m_MaxSize: 40
    m_Alignment: 0
    m_AlignByGeometry: 0
    m_RichText: 1
    m_HorizontalOverflow: 0
    m_VerticalOverflow: 0
    m_LineSpacing: 1
  m_Text: "<b><color=#ffff00>Welcome to the UMA Dynamic Character Avatar System</color></b>
    \n\nAt the heart of the system is the <b><color=#ffff00>DynamicCharacterAvatar</color></b>
    object.\n\n<i>A DynamicCharacterAvatar can be of any Race or Sex, and can be
    changed on the fly. The Wardrobe specifies what the avatar is wearing. The Wardrobe
    Slots are defined on the race. The system manages the wardrobe so the avatar
    only has one wardrobe item per slot.\n\nWardrobe parts are simply recipes. They
    can contain as many UMA slots and overlays as needed, and these are all merged
    into the base race recipe as needed.\n</i>\n\nTo create a recipe, right click
    in your project, and select \"Create / UMA Text Recipe\".\n\nSet the type of
    recipe to \"Wardrobe\". Set the wardrobe slot (Where it goes on the model). Set
    the races that can use the wardrobe item, and then define the recipe like normal
    by adding Slots and Overlays.\n\nA wardrobe item can <b>suppress</b> other wardrobe
    items (For example, putting on a long robe, can hide the clothing in the legs
    slot).\n\nA wardrobe item can also <b>hide</b> some of the UMA slots that make
    up the base model. For example, putting on gloves can hide the hands (so they
    don't poke through)."
